WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.153
You have probably done this yourself.

2
00:00:02.173 --> 00:00:05.643
You work out every piece of a hard problem,
and still get the answer wrong,

3
00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:08.620
because one piece never makes it into the
final step.

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.950
Language models do the same, even when we hand
them the answer to every step.

5
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:18.415
This is OracleLadder, our NeurIPS 2026 paper
on where LLM math reasoning breaks.

6
00:00:19.465 --> 00:00:21.175
Here is a short geometry problem.

7
00:00:21.195 --> 00:00:23.706
Line p is y = 2x − 1.

8
00:00:23.726 --> 00:00:29.197
Through the point (2, 3) on p, draw the line q
perpendicular to p.

9
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:34.418
Find the area of the convex quadrilateral that
p, q, and the two axes enclose.

10
00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:38.268
Qwen3-8B, a 2025 model, with thinking turned
off,

11
00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:40.688
gets it wrong in all eight attempts.

12
00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:42.661
But which part did it fail?

13
00:00:43.265 --> 00:00:47.265
The answer decides what to fix, whether you
train reasoning models,

14
00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:49.325
compare them, or build benchmarks.

15
00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:53.299
A benchmark score records only the final
answer.

16
00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:58.407
It cannot tell whether the model failed to
plan, to execute a step,

17
00:00:58.427 --> 00:01:02.006
to combine correct steps, or hit a step out of
reach.

18
00:01:02.692 --> 00:01:06.828
So we hold the problem fixed, and control what
the model is told.

19
00:01:06.848 --> 00:01:09.516
A strong teacher model, GPT-5.4 Thinking,

20
00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.467
reads the problem and its reference solution,

21
00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:15.090
and splits the solution into milestones:

22
00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.306
small standalone problems, each with a gold
answer we can check.

23
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.389
Automatic checks drop milestones that give the
answer away.

24
00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:28.153
The teacher never sees the models we test, so
every model gets the same roadmap.

25
00:01:28.757 --> 00:01:31.497
Here, milestone 1 finds line q.

26
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.005
Milestone 2 sets up where p crosses the
x-axis.

27
00:01:36.025 --> 00:01:38.869
The final step stays hidden, because its
answer is the solution.

28
00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:44.349
A symbolic verifier grades every answer, the
same way every time.

29
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:49.820
First, the milestone test. Can the model solve
each milestone on its own?

30
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.751
Qwen3 solves milestone 1 in 8 of 8 attempts,
and milestone 2 in 8 of 8.

31
00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:56.364
It passes.

32
00:01:57.046 --> 00:01:59.987
Then we climb a ladder of help on the full
problem.

33
00:02:00.007 --> 00:02:02.866
Rung 1: no help. 0 of 8.

34
00:02:02.886 --> 00:02:06.299
Rung 2: we add the roadmap. 0 of 8.

35
00:02:07.112 --> 00:02:13.346
Rung 3: the roadmap, plus both milestone
answers. Still 0 of 8.

36
00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:16.209
It can solve each step alone.

37
00:02:16.229 --> 00:02:18.438
It is handed the answer to every milestone.

38
00:02:18.458 --> 00:02:20.029
It still cannot finish.

39
00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:24.247
We call this a composition gap: every
milestone passes,

40
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:27.258
yet no rung of help solves the problem.

41
00:02:27.943 --> 00:02:29.653
What does this failure look like?

42
00:02:29.673 --> 00:02:31.794
We saved and read every answer it gave with

43
00:02:31.814 --> 00:02:34.459
the roadmap and both milestone answers in
hand.

44
00:02:34.479 --> 00:02:37.664
Every attempt finds all four axis intercepts
correctly.

45
00:02:37.684 --> 00:02:42.431
But every attempt leaves out the corner (2,
3), where p and q meet.

46
00:02:42.451 --> 00:02:45.063
One attempt even computes that point,

47
00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:48.636
then drops it, because it is not on an axis.

48
00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:52.928
The most common answer is 65/4, the area of
the wrong shape.

49
00:02:53.532 --> 00:02:58.365
The real quadrilateral keeps that corner. Its
area is 19/4.

50
00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:01.009
Every step is right. The assembly is wrong.

51
00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.022
Crossing the two tests gives five gaps.

52
00:03:05.042 --> 00:03:07.428
If the model passes the milestone test,

53
00:03:07.448 --> 00:03:10.347
and the roadmap is enough, it is a roadmap
gap.

54
00:03:11.044 --> 00:03:14.492
If it needs the answers, a milestone-execution
gap.

55
00:03:15.096 --> 00:03:17.394
If nothing works, a composition gap.

56
00:03:17.998 --> 00:03:22.022
If a milestone fails on its own, help can fill
that step:

57
00:03:22.042 --> 00:03:26.299
a missing-milestone gap. Or nothing works: a
capability gap.

58
00:03:26.999 --> 00:03:31.182
We ran OracleLadder on 354 NuminaMath
problems,

59
00:03:31.202 --> 00:03:37.683
and six open models from 2024 and 2025, from
8B to 671B parameters.

60
00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:42.589
For every model, the composition gap is the
largest of the five gaps,

61
00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:44.963
covering 33–48% of problems.

62
00:03:44.983 --> 00:03:48.477
Our verifier is strict, so some of these are
grading errors.

63
00:03:49.174 --> 00:03:54.150
After removing problems that an LLM review
flags as grading errors,

64
00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.885
it still covers 24–37%.

65
00:03:56.581 --> 00:03:59.644
What remains mixes roadmaps that miss a step,

66
00:03:59.664 --> 00:04:03.501
with real failures to combine, like the one we
just saw.

67
00:04:04.694 --> 00:04:07.275
One accuracy number hides real differences.

68
00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:12.446
DeepSeek-V3.1 has the higher per-attempt
accuracy:

69
00:04:12.466 --> 00:04:16.481
28.4%, against 25.7% for gpt-oss-20b.

70
00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:22.180
But gpt-oss solves more problems in at least
one of eight tries,

71
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:28.611
161 against 143, and milestone help widens the
lead, to 214 against 183.

72
00:04:29.299 --> 00:04:32.402
Is the roadmap real information, or just more
text?

73
00:04:32.422 --> 00:04:37.774
On AIME 2024 and 2025, a matched roadmap
recovers 45% of failed problems.

74
00:04:37.794 --> 00:04:42.735
A roadmap from another problem, or a generic
one, recovers 13%.

75
00:04:42.755 --> 00:04:46.575
The pattern holds with a second teacher, and
in code generation.

76
00:04:47.268 --> 00:04:48.630
Three takeaways.

77
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.869
Solving each step does not mean a model can
combine them.

78
00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:55.642
Measure how much help a model needs, not only
whether it is right.

79
00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:58.013
And separate grading errors, missing steps,

80
00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:01.981
and composition failures, because each points
to a different fix.

81
00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:06.540
Data, roadmaps, prompts, and code are open.
Come find us at our poster.

